We find that \(\sim 3\%\) of these Monte Carlo generated \(\Theta^{+}\)'s are successfully reconstructed. The reconstructed width and the mass is consistent with the Monte Carlo input. The \(\rm p_{T}\) vs rapidity of the input \(\Theta^{+}\) and those that are then reconstructed in the TPC are shown in Figure 7.4. The TPC acceptance and the efficiency of the particle reconstruction can be calculated from these two histograms as their ratio. As can be seen from Figure 7.4-b, the TPC acceptance is limited to \(|y|<1\). Low efficiencies are found for low \(\rm p_{T}\) particles (\(\rm p_{T}<0.5\) GeV/c) due to tracking inefficiency and high \(\rm p_{T}\) particles (\(\rm p_{T}>2\) GeV/c) due to cuts applied to purify the particle identification to minimize the backgrounds.

