### 7.5 Comparison of \(\Theta^{++}\) and \(\Lambda(1520)\) Production

The only difference between the decays products of the well known \(\Lambda(1520)\) resonance and that of the \(\Theta^{++}\) pentaquark is the charge of the K meson decay product. Since the \(\Lambda(1520)\) is neutral, it decays into a proton and \(K^{-}\) meson, while the \(\Theta^{++}\) is predicted to decay into a proton and \(K^{+}\) meson. A study of the \(\Lambda(1520)\) with cuts identical to those applied for the \(\Theta^{++}\) decay candidates, with the exception of the charge of the K meson, is used to compare the hypothesized \(\Theta^{++}\) pentaquark signal with the \(\Lambda(1520)\) (See Table 7.5).

The invariant mass distributions of the \(\Lambda(1520)\) and the mixed event background are presented in Figure 7.17-a. The mixed event background subtracted \(\Lambda(1520)\) peak and the correlated leftover background is fit with a Breit-Wigner plus a linear distribution (see Figure 7.17-b).

